  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 109 PM MDT Wed Oct 7 2026\n\nAfternoon satellite imagery shows virtually no cloud cover \nacross eastern Idaho as quiet and abnormally warm October \nweather continues. A stable synoptic regime will maintain this \npattern through the end of the workweek, with only subtle day- \nto-day temperature fluctuations and no appreciable chances for \nprecipitation. Daytime highs will run well above seasonal \nclimatological norms, accompanied by light winds and clear \novernight skies.\n\nWeather conditions begin to transition drastically on Saturday \nas a potent upper-level trough approaches the Intermountain \nWest. Surface winds will pick up noticeably during the day as \nthe regional pressure gradient tightens. Increased cloud cover \nand wind mixing will initiate a cooling trend, though daytime \nmaximum temperatures will still manage to hold a few degrees \nabove seasonal averages across most valleys. Cloud cover and \nprecipitation probabilities will increase rapidly during the \nsecond half of Saturday, spreading from southwest to northeast.\n\nA major airmass change overspreads eastern Idaho for the second\nhalf of the weekend into early next week, dropping temperatures\nwell below seasonal normals. Operational models have trended \ntoward consolidating a cutoff low over central California and \nNevada Sunday into Monday, though ensemble guidance maintains \nminor variance regarding the final track.\n\nAssuming this cutoff solution materializes, a moist southerly-\nto-southwesterly flow will become locked over eastern Idaho,\nmaintaining persistent precipitation through early next week.\nAdding to the precipitation potential will be an influx of\ntropical moisture from the remnants of Tropical Cyclone Rachel.\nThis rich moisture tap supports widespread heavy precipitation\nacross much of eastern Idaho, with current QPF forecasts calling\nfor 0.50 to 1.50 inches across lower-elevation valleys and the\neastern highlands, and 0.25 to 0.75 inches across the central\nmountains. While some uncertainty remains regarding the exact \ntrack and timing of the cutoff low and thus precip amounts -  \nconfidence is very high in a prolonged transition to a cool, wet\nautumn pattern \u2014 likely signaling an end to our late-summer-\nlike weather for the season. Below-normal temperatures and \nperiodic rain and mountain snow showers will persist well into \nnext week. Lower-elevation valley snow looks less likely with \nthis iteration of the forecast, but higher-terrain snowfall \nremains expected. Forecast updates will continue to refine snow \nlevels and QPF totals as guidance converges over the coming \ndays.",
